      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Edithvale Primary School began as a wooden building that burned in 1930, requiring over 40 firemen to extinguish the blaze.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Edithvale",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "During World War II, the town hosted the Edithvale Naval Air Station, a training centre for Australian naval pilots.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        14,
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://edithvale.vic.guide/History",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The Edithvale railway station opened in 1929, six years after the primary school, and was rebuilt in 2021 when the level crossing was removed.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12,
        13,
        16
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Edithvale",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Edithvale’s historic bathing boxes line the beach, a feature that made the suburb famous for its pure white sand and seaside charm.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
